Rollback load: keep compiled code on pages the load did not change

Every rollback load called invalidate_all_pages(): all compiled blocks discarded, two sweeps over
the million-entry page tables, then the re-run pays for recompiling. On a Switch that was about
9 ms of an 11 ms load. A rollback goes back a few frames and the game code in RAM has almost never
changed, so the load now notes, before RDRAM is overwritten, which pages holding compiled code
differ from the incoming state, and afterwards invalidates only those (invalidate_block, what a
guest write to the page does). Code from outside RDRAM is always dropped. Used only for rollback
buffer loads whose TLB entries are unchanged; everything else takes the full path as before.
The re-run after a load is verified exact (RDRAM hash of every frame) by znx-bench.

--- a/mupen64plus-core/src/device/r4300/new_dynarec/new_dynarec.c
+++ b/mupen64plus-core/src/device/r4300/new_dynarec/new_dynarec.c
@@ -2994,6 +2994,48 @@
     if(page==0x80000) page=0xC0000;
   }
   tlb_speed_hacks();
+}
+
+// Rollback loads. A rollback puts RAM back a few frames; the code the game runs has almost never
+// changed in that time, yet invalidate_all_pages() throws every compiled block away and walks two
+// million page-table entries (about 9 ms on a Cortex-A57, on every load, plus the recompiling
+// after it). Instead: before the load overwrites RAM, note which pages that hold compiled code
+// differ from the incoming state; afterwards invalidate just those, exactly as a guest write to
+// them would. Blocks on untouched pages stay valid because the bytes they were compiled from are
+// the same. Only used when the TLB did not change (the caller checks).
+static unsigned char rollback_changed_page[2048];
+static int rollback_changed_noted;
+
+void new_dynarec_rollback_note_ram(const void* incoming_rdram)
+{
+  const unsigned char* now=(const unsigned char*)g_dev.rdram.dram;
+  const unsigned char* then=(const unsigned char*)incoming_rdram;
+  u_int page;
+  for(page=0;page<2048;page++)
+    rollback_changed_page[page]=(jump_in[page]||jump_out[page])&&memcmp(now+page*4096,then+page*4096,4096)!=0;
+  rollback_changed_noted=1;
+}
+
+int new_dynarec_rollback_invalidate_changed(void)
+{
+  u_int page;
+  int outside=0;
+  if(!rollback_changed_noted) return 0;
+  rollback_changed_noted=0;
+  for(page=0;page<2048;page++)
+    if(rollback_changed_page[page]) invalidate_block(0x80000+page);
+  // Code compiled from outside RDRAM (SP memory, PIF): the load replaces those too.
+  for(page=2048;page<4096;page++)
+    if(jump_in[page]||jump_out[page]) { invalidate_page(page); outside=1; }
+  if(outside) {
+    #if NEW_DYNAREC >= NEW_DYNAREC_ARM
+    do_clear_cache();
+    #endif
+    #ifdef USE_MINI_HT
+    memset(g_dev.r4300.new_dynarec_hot_state.mini_ht,-1,sizeof(g_dev.r4300.new_dynarec_hot_state.mini_ht));
+    #endif
+  }
+  return 1;
 }
 
